## Pre-edit checklist

**1. `git status --short`** — shown above: only `.claude-user/**` churn (excluded from scope). Source tree is clean.

**2. Files I will create/modify:**
- New: `artifacts/api-server/src/lib/todoist-sync/types.ts`
- New: `artifacts/api-server/src/lib/todoist-sync/delivery.ts`
- New: `artifacts/api-server/src/lib/todoist-sync/delivery.test.ts`
- New: `artifacts/api-server/vitest.config.ts` (required — no vitest config exists anywhere in the repo, confirmed by search)
- Modify: `artifacts/api-server/src/routes/todoist.ts`
- Modify: `artifacts/api-server/package.json` (add `vitest` devDependency + `test` script only)
- `pnpm-lock.yaml` will update as a side effect of `pnpm install` adding vitest (no vitest entry exists in the shared catalog either — I'll pin it directly in `api-server`'s own `devDependencies`, not touch `pnpm-workspace.yaml`)

No other file is required.

**3. Proposed public interface:**

```ts
// lib/todoist-sync/types.ts
export type TodoistItemType = "task" | "eblast";

export type DeliveryOutcome =
  | "created"
  | "updated"
  | "deleted"
  | "skipped_no_due_date"
  | "unlinked_remote_missing"
  | "failed";

export type CompletionAction = "closed" | "reopened" | "none";

export interface DeliveryResult {
  itemType: TodoistItemType;
  itemId: number;
  outcome: DeliveryOutcome;
  todoistTaskId: string | null;   // resulting local mapping after this attempt
  completionAction: CompletionAction;
  error?: string;
}

export interface SyncCounters {
  created: number;
  updated: number;
  deleted: number;
}

export function tallyOutcome(counters: SyncCounters, outcome: DeliveryOutcome): void;
```

```ts
// lib/todoist-sync/delivery.ts
export interface DeliveryDeps {
  todoistRequest: (method: string, path: string, body?: unknown) => Promise<unknown>;
  makeTodoistTask: (content: string, description: string, dueDate: string, projectId?: string) => object;
  persistTodoistTaskId: (itemType: TodoistItemType, itemId: number, todoistTaskId: string | null) => Promise<void>;
}

// Real implementation: todoist-client.ts functions + db.update(tasksTable/eblastsTable).
// Tests inject a fake DeliveryDeps — no network, no DB, no Todoist connector involved.
export const defaultDeliveryDeps: DeliveryDeps;

export async function deliverTask(
  task: typeof tasksTable.$inferSelect,
  showName: string,
  projectId: string,
  deps?: DeliveryDeps,
): Promise<DeliveryResult>;

export async function deliverEblast(
  eblast: typeof eblastsTable.$inferSelect,
  showName: string,
  projectId: string,
  deps?: DeliveryDeps,
): Promise<DeliveryResult>;
```

`deliverTask`/`deliverEblast` are thin wrappers that build the existing content/description strings unchanged, then call a shared internal (unexported) core that holds the create/update/complete/delete/404 logic once, so task and e-blast paths cannot diverge.

**4. Exact 404 behavior change vs. today:**

Today (`routes/todoist.ts` current `syncTask`/`syncEblast`): an update returning 404 sets `todoistTaskId = null` in memory and **falls through into the create branch in the same pass**, silently issuing a new `POST /api/v1/tasks` and persisting a brand-new remote id — i.e. it auto-recreates.

New behavior: an update returning 404 clears the local `todoistTaskId` (persists `null` via `deps.persistTodoistTaskId`), returns `outcome: "unlinked_remote_missing"`, and **stops** — no create call is issued in this same delivery attempt. The local task/e-blast row itself is left otherwise untouched. Recreating it is deliberately left to a future, explicitly-approved repair action (per your Phase 2 rule 7) — nothing in this PR re-creates it automatically, not even on the item's next ordinary edit.

**5. Confirmation — no automatic sync added:** This PR adds zero calls into `delivery.ts` from `tasks.ts`/`eblasts.ts` (both files are untouched), zero `todoist_sync_events` reads/writes, zero timers/polling/schedulers. `routes/todoist.ts`'s existing manual `POST /export/todoist/sync` route remains the only caller of the new delivery functions, triggered the same way it is today — a user click.

Proceeding to implementation.

